1. Introduction:
Security monitoring is a core responsibility of a security operations center (SOC). It involves continuously observing systems, networks, and applications to detect suspicious activities, unauthorized access, and violations of organizational security policies. Effective monitoring enables early detection of cyber threats and reduces the impact of security incidents.
2. Objectives:
The objectives of this task were:
To understand the purpose and importance of security monitoring in a SOC.
To identify anomalies, unauthorized access attempts, and policy violations.
To gain hands-on exposure to SIEM and network traffic analysis tools.
To understand key SOC performance metrics such as detection accuracy and response time.
3. Security Monitoring Basics:
3.1 Objectives of Security Monitoring:
Security monitoring aims to:
Detect anomalous behavior and deviations from normal activity.
Identify unauthorized access attempts.
Monitor violations of security policies.
Enable early detection of attacks and suspicious behavior.
Support incident response and forensic investigations.
3.2 Tools Used for Security Monitoring:
The following tools are commonly used in SOC environments:
Splunk – SIEM platform for log aggregation, correlation, and alerting.
Elastic – Open-source SIEM used for log analysis and threat detection.
Wireshark – Network traffic analysis tool for packet inspection.
3.3 Key Security Monitoring Metrics:
The following metrics were studied to evaluate SOC effectiveness:
False Positives—Benign activities incorrectly flagged as malicious.
False Negatives—Malicious activities that go undetected.
Mean Time to Detect (MTTD) – Average time taken to identify a security incident.
Understanding these metrics helps improve detection rules and monitoring accuracy.
4. Learning Methodology:
4.1 Lab Environment Setup:
A lab environment was set up using open-source monitoring tools, primarily Elastic SIEM, to simulate a real-world SOC monitoring setup. Logs and network traffic data were ingested into the SIEM for analysis.
4.2 Network Traffic Analysis:
Sample network traffic logs were analyzed to identify:
Unusual traffic spikes.
Suspicious IP addresses.
Unauthorized connection attempts.
Abnormal protocol usage.
Packet-level inspection was performed using Wireshark to understand traffic patterns and identify anomalies.
4.3 Attack Scenario Analysis:
Pre-recorded attack datasets were used to practice detection and analysis.
The boss of the SOC datasets was analyzed to simulate real-world attack scenarios.
These datasets provided realistic logs representing attacks such as brute-force attempts, lateral movement, and suspicious network activity.
5. Observations and Key Learnings:
Continuous monitoring is essential for early threat detection.
SIEM platforms provide centralized visibility across logs and network data.
Network traffic analysis complements log-based monitoring.
Understanding false positives and false negatives improves alert quality.
Measuring MTTD helps evaluate SOC efficiency and detection maturity.
6. Conclusion:
This task provided foundational knowledge in security monitoring practices used in a SOC environment. Through hands-on analysis of logs, network traffic, and simulated attack datasets, practical understanding was gained in identifying suspicious activities and evaluating monitoring effectiveness. These skills are critical for performing real-world SOC analyst duties.
